# Files whose BYTES are an identity, not merely whose content is.
#
# The criterion checksum frozen in docs/MERGE_CRITERION_PREREGISTRATION.md, and
# the `criteria_sha256` a run records in its provenance, are both sha256 of the
# file exactly as it sits on disk. Git's default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows
# rewrites line endings at checkout, so the same committed file hashes
# differently there. Measured on a Windows clone: criteria.v2.yaml checks out
# with 416 CRLF and hashes to e35ac653..., while the committed blob is LF and
# hashes to 8af0a879... -- which is the value the preregistration document
# records.
#
# Two harms, and the second is the worse one. The preregistration check -- the
# mechanism that exists to make a silent criterion change impossible -- fires as
# a false alarm on every Windows clone, which teaches a reader to dismiss exactly
# the alarm it was built to raise. And a provenance record written on Windows
# disagrees with one written on Linux about which criteria produced the run,
# for two runs that used the identical file.
#
# Pinned rather than left to `core.autocrlf` because the property has to hold on
# a machine whose git is configured by somebody else.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.tsv  text eol=lf
